From d70e9d71caba2fa378423851463ca435b578b6cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DeepSeek Harness <agent@local>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 00:51:54 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Video VAE: run the ViT decoder in BF16 so MPS uses the tensor
 cores

The MiniMax-H3 video VAE decoder was a 36-layer / 2048-wide ViT that ran
entirely in FP32.  MPS FP32 matmul is a plain FP32 SIMD path, so the
Apple tensor cores sat idle: a micro-benchmark at the decoder's real
shapes measured 6.2 TFLOP/s, which is the M5 Pro's FP32 SIMD peak.

Switching the decoder to BF16 moves the same GEMMs onto the tensor
cores (26 TFLOP/s measured, ~4x) and cuts the 960x544x124 decode from
159.45 s to 47.33 s (3.37x), 409.95 s to 316.93 s end to end, with the
video VAE peak allocation halved from 9.37 GiB to 4.68 GiB.  Relative
error against the FP32 decoder is 0.0048, versus the 0.02 bound VPIPE
uses for its own decoder.

Two new BF16 primitives fill the last gaps in the BF16 path:
h3_scale_add_bf16 and h3_video_qkv_rope_bf16 mirror their FP32
counterparts line for line, including the half-split RoPE sign handling
and the QK RMS epsilon placement.  Both are registered in the pipeline
warm-up list.

The checkpoint stores all 560 decoder tensors as FP32, so
h3_weight_load_f32_as_bf16 converts them on the host with
round-to-nearest-even before upload.  That costs 0.18 s for the full
9.7 GiB set against 112 s saved, and resident weights drop from
9.16 GiB to 4.58 GiB.

Also align the spatial tile size with the checkpoint.  The old code
auto-tuned it to 304 px, but the config specifies vae_tile_size 256, and
the RoPE grid is normalised by the tile's own latent extent, so 304 px
silently evaluates a different position encoding than the model was
trained with.  256 px is now the default; H3_VAE_TILE_PIXELS still
overrides it.

H3_VAE_PRECISION=f32 restores the original FP32 path for A/B runs and
regression testing.
